+# BLOCKED: the `test_sla` latency target is not physically achievable
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_wordfreq.WordFreqTests.test_sla` requires `top_words()` to process the
+600,000-word (~4.08 MB) reference corpus in **under 1 ms** on CPython 3.12.5
+(macOS, aarch64). It is not possible to meet that budget honestly on this
+hardware and runtime. The test is left unchanged (it is the signed-off SLA, not
+a test to loosen), so the suite still reports this one timing failure.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+Even the mandatory, non-optional stages exceed 1 ms before any counting work
+starts. Measured on the exact corpus from `corpus()` in `test_wordfreq.py`:
+
+| Stage | Best observed |
+| --- | ---: |
+| `text.lower()` only | **1.54 ms** |
+| `re.findall(r"[a-z']+", text.lower())` | 56.3 ms |
+| `Counter(re.findall(...))` (tokenize + count) | 95.5 ms |
+| `Counter(text.lower().split())` (cheapest possible tokenize + count) | 56.2 ms |
+| final `top_words()` implementation (CPython 3.12.5) | **65.2 ms** |
+
+The contract of `top_words` is case-insensitive matching of runs of letters and
+apostrophes. That requires reading all 4,083,137 bytes and casefolding them.
+`text.lower()` alone takes ~1.5 ms — 1.5x the entire SLA — with no tokenizing,
+hashing, or sorting performed yet. The counting step must then hash roughly
+600,000 tokens. A 1 ms budget would require >600 million tokens/second
+(~4 GB/s of token hashing) inside a Python function call.
+
+This is not merely a CPython limitation. A native C lower-bound check
+(`-O3 -march=native`) on the same corpus measured:
+
+| Native stage | Best observed |
+| --- | ---: |
+| copy + lowercase 4 MB | 0.08 ms |
+| tokenize + hash + count, no case folding | **8.99 ms** |
+| tokenize + hash + count with case folding | **11.44 ms** |
+
+So even an optimized C implementation needs ~9x the SLA for the token-counting
+step alone. Reaching 1 ms would require an exact hash/count of 600,000 tokens in
+under ~1.7 ns per token including the scan, which no realistic implementation
+achieves.
+
+The test's own oracle (`Counter(text.lower().split())`) is computed outside the
+timed region, but the same work inside `top_words` cannot cost less.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+The real performance bug was algorithmic: the original code did linear scans
+(`w in seen`, `seen.index(w)`) for every word, i.e. O(words x vocabulary),
+taking **6.37 s**. It now normalizes the lowercased text at C level
+(`encode`/`translate`), splits it, and counts with `collections.Counter`,
+preserving the documented semantics exactly (same `[a-z']+` word rule,
+casefold, `(-count, word)` ordering, top `n`, verified against the old
+implementation on 5,000 fuzzed strings):
+
+- `test_basic`, `test_case_and_apostrophes`, `test_ties_alphabetical`: pass.
+- `test_sla` correctness (`assertEqual(got, want)`): passes.
+- `test_sla` runtime: ~65 ms, a **~98x speedup**, but still ~65x over budget.
+
+## What would unblock it
+
+One of these must change, and it is a product/engineering decision rather than
+a code fix:
+
+1. Set a realistic SLA for CPython (e.g. ~150 ms for this corpus), or
+2. Lower the corpus size in the SLA test proportionally, or
+3. Replace the implementation with a maintained native extension and accept
+   the build/deployment cost — even then, 1 ms for 4 MB is not realistic.
+
+I did not memoize/precompute the seeded corpus, weaken the assertion, or return
+partial/fake results: those would make the check green without meeting the SLA
+for real articles, which is exactly the workaround the task rules out.
